{sov: Notes}
Country Blues Staple: Originated by Bukka White in 1937, covered widely by
Fred McDowell, RL Burnside, Led Zeppelin ("Hats Off to (Roy) Harper"), and
North Mississippi Allstars.

Hill Country Driving Groove: Often played in Open E or Open D tuning with a
slide, driven by a relentless, hypnotically repetitive thumb-bass rhythm on the
low strings.

12-Bar Form Adaptability: While traditionally performed as a loose 12-bar
blues, Hill Country arrangements often stay parked on a heavy single-chord E7
vamp for long stretches before hitting the IV (A7) and V (B7) turnarounds.

Slide & Percussive Accents: Accent the high-string slide snaps on the offbeats
and keep the low E string thump steady to lock in with the drum groove.
{eov}